2025-05-01 03:21:59 - Thread-1356 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns for the question  
• orders.employeeid – connects an order to the employee who handled it  
• orders.orderid      – just a key if we need to count orders  
• orders.requireddate – the “promise‑by” date (used to judge lateness)  
• orders.shippeddate  – the actual ship date (late if shippeddate ≥ requireddate)  
• employees.employeeid – lets us pull additional employee data or be sure every
  employee we quote really exists in the employees table  

Typical conditions we will need  
• orders.shippeddate IS NOT NULL  (ignore orders that haven’t shipped)  
• orders.shippeddate >= orders.requireddate  → “late order”  
• GROUP BY orders.employeeid  
• HAVING COUNT(*) > 50              → only employees with >50 total orders  

Below are ten exploratory SQL statements (from very simple “peek” queries to
a full solution) that one could run in SQLite to understand the data and finally
return the requested result.  Each query is limited to 20 rows so it is safe to
run in an open notebook environment.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at which employees have any orders at all
SELECT DISTINCT "employeeid"
FROM "orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. How many total orders does each employee have?
SELECT "employeeid",
       COUNT(*) AS "total_orders"
FROM   "orders"
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
ORDER  BY "total_orders" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same as #2 but keep only employees with >50 orders
SELECT "employeeid",
       COUNT(*) AS "total_orders"
FROM   "orders"
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 50
ORDER  BY "total_orders" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Look at a sample of late orders (shipped on/after required)
SELECT "orderid",
       "employeeid",
       "requireddate",
       "shippeddate"
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
  AND  "shippeddate" >= "requireddate"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Count late orders per employee (no total filter yet)
SELECT "employeeid",
       COUNT(*) AS "late_orders"
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
  AND  "shippeddate" >= "requireddate"
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
ORDER  BY "late_orders" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Employees with >50 orders and their total order counts, plus name
SELECT e."employeeid",
       e."firstname" || ' ' || e."lastname" AS "employee_name",
       COUNT(o."orderid") AS "total_orders"
FROM   "employees" AS e
JOIN   "orders"   AS o USING ("employeeid")
GROUP  BY e."employeeid"
HAVING COUNT(o."orderid") > 50
ORDER  BY "total_orders" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Compute both total orders and late orders for every employee
SELECT "employeeid",
       SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate") AS "late_orders",
       COUNT(*)                              AS "total_orders"
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add the late‑order percentage, still for everybody
SELECT "employeeid",
       SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate")        AS "late_orders",
       COUNT(*)                                     AS "total_orders",
       100.0 * SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate")
           / COUNT(*)                              AS "late_pct"
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
ORDER  BY "late_pct" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Keep only employees with >50 total orders, order by late %
SELECT "employeeid",
       SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate")        AS "late_orders",
       COUNT(*)                                     AS "total_orders",
       100.0 * SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate")
           / COUNT(*)                              AS "late_pct"
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY "employeeid"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 50
ORDER  BY "late_pct" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer – the top three employees (ID, late orders, % late)
WITH per_emp AS (
    SELECT "employeeid",
           SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate") AS "late_orders",
           COUNT(*)                             AS "total_orders",
           100.0 * SUM("shippeddate" >= "requireddate") / COUNT(*) AS "late_pct"
    FROM   "orders"
    WHERE  "shippeddate" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP  BY "employeeid"
    HAVING COUNT(*) > 50
)
SELECT "employeeid",
       "late_orders",
       ROUND("late_pct", 2) AS "late_pct"
FROM   per_emp
ORDER  BY "late_pct" DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

Running the last query will return exactly the three employees who (a) have more
than fifty orders in total and (b) exhibit the highest proportion of late
orders, together with the count of such late orders and the calculated
percentage.
